## Usage

### Modes
`mode` picks the material: `refraction` (the default), `glass`, `acrylic`, `blur` backdrop blur, `mask` translucent overlay, `mica`, `pure` solid (`flat` is an alias), or `plain`, which paints no material. Each material's pipeline and cost are in [Surface 8-Material Taxonomy & Gatekeepers](/docs/dev/concepts/surface-taxonomy).
:::TuffDemoWrapper{demo="BaseSurfaceModesDemo" code-lang="vue"}
---
code: |
  <template>
    <TxBaseSurface mode="pure">Pure</TxBaseSurface>
    <TxBaseSurface mode="mask" :opacity="0.6">Mask</TxBaseSurface>
    <TxBaseSurface mode="blur" :blur="8">Blur</TxBaseSurface>
    <TxBaseSurface mode="glass" :blur="12" :saturation="1.8">Glass</TxBaseSurface>
    <TxBaseSurface mode="refraction" :blur="11" :displace="0.8" :distortion-scale="-200">Refraction</TxBaseSurface>
    <TxBaseSurface mode="acrylic">Acrylic</TxBaseSurface>
    <TxBaseSurface mode="mica">Mica</TxBaseSurface>
    <TxBaseSurface mode="plain">Plain</TxBaseSurface>
  </template>
---
:::

### Advanced Lab
`filter*` props tune the filter layer, `refraction*` props the refraction layer, and `preset="card"` applies the card tuning.
:::TuffDemoWrapper{demo="BaseSurfaceAdvancedDemo" code-lang="vue"}
---
code: |
  <template>
    <TxBaseSurface
      mode="refraction"
      preset="card"
      :radius="18"
      :blur="24"
      :filter-saturation="1.6"
      refraction-profile="filmic"
      :refraction-strength="68"
      :refraction-angle="-24"
      :refraction-halo-opacity="0.42"
    >
      BaseSurface Advanced
    </TxBaseSurface>
  </template>
---
:::

### Fake Mode
`fake` paints the background on a pseudo-element, matching the existing `.fake-background` layering; slot content sits above it naturally. Under `pure` (`flat`) that base is opaque and under `mask` it follows the mask opacity; under `blur`, `acrylic`, `glass` and `refraction` it sits beneath the material at `opacity`, so the page behind still shows through to be blurred and refracted; `mica` paints its own opaque base and `plain` paints nothing, so neither takes it.
:::TuffDemoWrapper{demo="BaseSurfaceFakeDemo" code-lang="vue"}
---
code: |
  <template>
    <TxBaseSurface fake mode="mask" :opacity="0.5" :radius="12">
      <strong>Pseudo-element background</strong>
    </TxBaseSurface>
    <TxBaseSurface fake mode="pure" :radius="12" color="var(--tx-color-primary-light-9)">
      Custom color
    </TxBaseSurface>
  </template>
---
:::

### Motion Fallback
When a surface moves with its element, `backdrop-filter` samples lag behind the element's position, and the filter layers drag a misaligned patch of backdrop along. With `moving` set, `blur`, `glass` and `acrylic` degrade to `fallbackMode` and recover smoothly when it stops; `refraction` keeps its mode and fades its filter layers out, then back in once it stops.
:::TuffDemoWrapper{demo="BaseSurfaceFallbackDemo" code-lang="vue"}
---
code: |
  <template>
    <TxButton @click="animating = !animating">
      {{ animating ? 'Stop Motion' : 'Start Motion' }}
    </TxButton>

    <div
      :style="{ transform: animating ? 'translateX(16px) scale(0.92)' : 'none' }"
      style="transition: transform 1.4s;"
    >
      <TxBaseSurface mode="glass" :blur="12">No fallback</TxBaseSurface>
      <TxBaseSurface mode="glass" :blur="12" :moving="animating" fallback-mode="mask">
        With fallback
      </TxBaseSurface>
    </div>
  </template>
---
:::

### Raw vs BaseSurface
When the motion state isn't available, turn on `autoDetect` and the surface senses motion itself (see the next section).
:::TuffDemoWrapper{demo="BaseSurfaceMotionCompareDemo" code-lang="vue"}
---
code: |
  <template>
    <div
      :style="{ transform: animating ? 'translateX(20px)' : '' }"
      style="backdrop-filter: blur(12px); transition: transform 1.2s;"
    >
      Raw glass (breaks on move)
    </div>

    <TxBaseSurface mode="glass" :blur="12" :moving="animating" auto-detect fallback-mode="mask">
      BaseSurface (degrades to mask)
    </TxBaseSurface>
  </template>
---
:::

### Environment sensing
With `autoDetect` on, the surface combines five runtime signals: transforms on itself and its ancestors, the speed of a pressed pointer (above 1.2px/ms), window moves and resizes, a frame-budget probe, and power saving. The first three trigger the motion fallback, which starts to recover 140ms after the signal stops; under power saving or a `reduced` frame budget, `refraction`, `glass` and `acrylic` become `blur`, and under `minimal` they and `blur` become `flat`, returning to the declared material once the condition clears. How each signal is read is in [Adaptive Surface Deduction & 120Hz Fallback](/docs/dev/concepts/surface-deduction).

A page receives no event when its window moves, so the host forwards one; a host that knows the power state can override the battery API's reading:

```ts
import { notifySurfaceWindowMotion, setSurfacePowerSaving } from '@talex-touch/tuffex'

// Once per BrowserWindow move (and will-move) event the main process relays to the renderer
notifySurfaceWindowMotion()

// From Electron's powerMonitor, say; null hands it back to the Battery Status API
setSurfacePowerSaving(true)
```

### Flow light
`flowLight` lays a fixed brand-colour light ([FlowLight](/docs/dev/components/flow-light)) over the veil and under the edge, in any `mode`.
:::TuffDemoWrapper{demo="BaseSurfaceFlowLightDemo" code-lang="vue"}
---
code: |
  <template>
    <TxBaseSurface mode="refraction" flow-light="rim" :radius="14">
      Panel content
    </TxBaseSurface>
  </template>
---
:::

### System backdrop
A transparent Electron window lets the desktop show through its own system material (vibrancy on macOS, Mica on Windows), and a page's `backdrop-filter` cannot reach that material: the filter samples only the surface's own veil and lays the copy over that veil, so a 75% veil stacked twice is all but opaque and the desktop no longer shows through. `systemBackdrop` runs no `backdrop-filter` on the surface: the glass layer doesn't render and the filter layer keeps only its tint, leaving the blur to the system; the veil, mask, flow light and rim draw as usual. With nothing sampled from the page, `moving` and `autoDetect` have no effect either.

```vue
<!-- core-app's CoreBox: a transparent window, its surface over the system material -->
<TxBaseSurface system-backdrop fake color="var(--tx-fill-color)" :opacity="0.75" />
```

### Best Practices

- Prefer `TxCard` for product containers, since it owns container semantics and interaction; use `TxBaseSurface` only to tune material, fallback, or refraction directly.
- Pass `moving` when the parent owns the animation state; use `autoDetect` only for motion whose state you can't reach, or to switch to a lighter material under power saving and dropped frames.
- Keep `fallbackMode="mask"` so `blur` / `glass` / `acrylic` content stays readable in motion; use `pure` only when a flat color is acceptable.
- Tune refraction through `refractionStrength`, `refractionProfile`, and `refractionTone`; keep RGB channel offsets for visual experiments.
- Use `refractionRenderer="css"` only where its lower optical fidelity is acceptable.

## API Reference

### Props

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
|------|------|---------|-------------|
| `mode` | `'pure' \| 'mask' \| 'blur' \| 'glass' \| 'refraction' \| 'acrylic' \| 'mica' \| 'flat' \| 'plain'` | `'refraction'` | Material: refraction, glass, acrylic, backdrop blur, mask, mica, solid (`flat` is the same as `pure`), or no material. |
| `radius` | `string \| number` | - | Corner radius; inherits from the parent when unset. |
| `color` | `string` | - | Base color for the solid and mask layers, the acrylic tint and the mica base. |
| `opacity` | `number` | `0.75` | Opacity in `mask` mode (0–1); `acrylic`'s tint takes 0.8 of it; in fake mode, also the opacity of the base colour under the `blur` / `acrylic` / `glass` / `refraction` material. |
| `fallbackMaskOpacity` | `number` | - | Opacity when degraded to `mask` (0–1). |
| `blur` | `number` | `10` | Filter-layer blur radius in px; `acrylic` takes three times it. |
| `filterSaturation` | `number` | `1.5` | Filter-layer saturation. |
| `filterContrast` | `number` | `1` | Filter-layer contrast. |
| `filterBrightness` | `number` | `1` | Filter-layer brightness. |
| `saturation` | `number` | `1.8` | Glass-layer saturation for glass and refraction. |
| `brightness` | `number` | `70` | Glass-layer brightness for glass and refraction; `<= 3` is read as a multiplier. |
| `backgroundOpacity` | `number` | `0` | Background opacity of the glass layer. |
| `borderWidth` | `number` | `0.07` | Edge width factor of the glass layer. |
| `displace` | `number` | `0.5` | Refraction displacement amount. |
| `distortionScale` | `number` | `-180` | Refraction distortion scale. |
| `redOffset` / `greenOffset` / `blueOffset` | `number` | `0 / 10 / 20` | RGB channel offsets that control dispersion. |
| `xChannel` / `yChannel` | `'R' \| 'G' \| 'B'` | `'R' / 'G'` | Channels sampled by the displacement map. |
| `mixBlendMode` | `string` | `'difference'` | Refraction blend mode. |
| `refractionStrength` | `number` | `62` | Refraction strength, 0–100. |
| `refractionProfile` | `'soft' \| 'filmic' \| 'cinematic'` | `'filmic'` | Refraction style preset. |
| `refractionTone` | `'mist' \| 'balanced' \| 'vivid'` | `'balanced'` | Refraction tone: `vivid` is clearer, `mist` softer. |
| `refractionAngle` | `number` | `-24` | Main dispersion angle in degrees. |
| `refractionLightX` / `refractionLightY` | `number` | - | Light anchor (0–1). |
| `refractionHaloOpacity` | `number` | - | Halo opacity (0–1); the built-in filmic model applies when unset. |
| `overlayOpacity` | `number` | `0` | Extra mask opacity for non-mask modes. |
| `flowLight` | `'corners' \| 'rim' \| 'aurora' \| false` | - | The flow light's variant; absent or `false` is off, any `mode` can have it. |
| `flowLightIntensity` | `number` | `1` | Flow light strength, multiplying the theme opacity. |
| `preset` | `'default' \| 'card'` | `'default'` | Visual preset; `card` applies the card tuning. |
| `refractionRenderer` | `'svg' \| 'css'` | `'svg'` | Refraction renderer. |
| `systemBackdrop` | `boolean` | `false` | The surface sits on the window's own system material (vibrancy or Mica in a transparent Electron window): it runs no `backdrop-filter` and leaves the blur to the system; `moving` and `autoDetect` have no effect. |
| `moving` | `boolean` | `false` | Marks the surface as moving, which triggers the fallback. |
| `fallbackMode` | `'pure' \| 'mask' \| 'flat'` | `'mask'` | Mode used while moving; `flat` is the same as `pure`. |
| `settleDelay` | `number` | `150` | Delay in ms before recovery after motion ends; never shorter than `transitionDuration`. |
| `autoDetect` | `boolean` | `false` | Degrades by environment: falls back in motion when the surface or an ancestor transforms, a pressed pointer moves fast, or the window moves or resizes; switches to a lighter material under power saving or dropped frames. |
| `transitionDuration` | `number` | `299` | Recovery transition duration in ms. |
| `fake` | `boolean` | `false` | Renders the background on a pseudo-element. |
| `fakeIndex` | `number` | `0` | z-index of the pseudo-element layer. |
| `tag` | `string` | `'div'` | Root element tag. |

